{
  "id": "m9jl35c",
  "title": "Chapter 43: Planning in India",
  "order": 43,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h9c2is9",
      "content": "Economic planning refers to the systematic use of resources by the government to achieve specific economic and social objectives. In India, economic planning was introduced to promote rapid economic growth, reduce poverty, increase employment and achieve balanced regional development."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Planning Commission",
          "value": "1950",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "Five-Year Plans",
          "value": "12 Plans",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "NITI Aayog Established",
          "value": "2015",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "First Five-Year Plan",
          "value": "1951–1956",
          "icon": "clock"
        }
      ],
      "id": "vg7puar"
    },
    {
      "id": "fn3ztom",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Economic Planning"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Economic planning in India aims at growth, modernization, self-reliance, and social justice.",
        "The Planning Commission was established in 1950 to formulate India's Five-Year Plans.",
        "India executed 12 Five-Year Plans prior to structural reforms in national planning.",
        "NITI Aayog replaced the Planning Commission on January 1, 2015, acting as an extra-constitutional think tank.",
        "NITI Aayog operates on the principle of bottom-up development and cooperative federalism."
      ],
      "id": "ivqh61k"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6qybz7h",
      "content": "• Economic planning is the process through which the government decides how resources will be used."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e317dgq",
      "content": "• It involves setting targets and policies for economic development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f8ljj6f",
      "content": "• It aims to achieve balanced growth and social welfare."
    },
    {
      "id": "x4dci0e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Objectives of Economic Planning in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k5dl40f",
      "content": "• Economic growth and development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6d35irm",
      "content": "• Reduction of poverty and unemployment."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s816g3p",
      "content": "• Modernization of economy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dltwawx",
      "content": "• Self-reliance and economic independence."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ufyb2ms",
      "content": "• Balanced regional development."
    },
    {
      "id": "sd0j4ug",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Planning Commission"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b4i2hyu",
      "content": "The Planning Commission was established in 1950 to formulate Five-Year Plans for India's economic development. It worked under the chairmanship of the Prime Minister."
    },
    {
      "id": "k87fuim",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Functions of Planning Commission"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "00pksev",
      "content": "• Formulate Five-Year Plans."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g1c4b2x",
      "content": "• Assess resources of the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sfbqkgt",
      "content": "• Determine priorities for development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n4k371u",
      "content": "• Monitor implementation of plans."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Planning Commission",
        "NITI Aayog"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nature",
          "Extra-constitutional body (Formed 1950)",
          "Think Tank / Advisory Body (Formed 2015)"
        ],
        [
          "Approach",
          "Top-Down Planning",
          "Bottom-Up Planning (Cooperative Federalism)"
        ],
        [
          "Financial Powers",
          "Power to allocate funds to ministries and states",
          "No power to allocate funds (handled by Finance Ministry)"
        ],
        [
          "State Role",
          "Limited representation in policy design",
          "Active involvement of States in the Governing Council"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "5jxp1mt"
    },
